 <h2>The Problem: Core, Friction &amp; Windage</h2>
 <p>Copper and brush losses fall straight out of the circuit. But <strong>core loss</strong> (hysteresis + eddy currents in the iron) and <strong>mechanical loss</strong> — <strong>friction</strong> (bearings and brushes) and <strong>windage</strong> (air drag on the spinning rotor) — cannot be computed from any nameplate resistance. Lumped together they are the <strong>rotational losses</strong>, and in practice they are <em>measured</em>, then modeled.</p>
 <h3>The no-load test</h3>
-<p>Run the motor with <strong>no mechanical load</strong>. The shaft output is then essentially zero, and the copper and stray losses are negligible, so the converted power is spent almost entirely on rotational losses: <strong>P<sub>rot</sub> = P<sub>core</sub> + P<sub>mech</sub> ≈ E<sub>A0</sub>·I<sub>A0</sub></strong>, where E<sub>A0</sub> = V<sub>T0</sub> − I<sub>A0</sub>R<sub>A</sub> − V<sub>BD</sub> (equivalently V<sub>T0</sub>I<sub>A0</sub> − I<sub>A0</sub>²R<sub>A</sub> − V<sub>BD</sub>I<sub>A0</sub>). That single measurement anchors the loss model. The <em>Run No-Load Test</em> button performs exactly this on the current machine.</p>
+<p>Run the motor with <strong>no mechanical load</strong>. The shaft output is then essentially zero, and the copper and stray losses are negligible, so the converted power is spent almost entirely on rotational losses: <strong>P<sub>rot</sub> = P<sub>core</sub> + P<sub>mech</sub> ≈ E<sub>A0</sub>·I<sub>A0</sub></strong> (equivalently P<sub>rot</sub> ≈ V<sub>T0</sub>I<sub>A0</sub> − I<sub>A0</sub>²R<sub>A</sub> − V<sub>BD</sub>I<sub>A0</sub>), where E<sub>A0</sub> = V<sub>T0</sub> − I<sub>A0</sub>R<sub>A</sub> − V<sub>BD</sub>. That single measurement anchors the loss model. The <em>Run No-Load Test</em> button performs exactly this on the current machine.</p>
